---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Card, Grid, Timeline, TimelineItem } from "webcoreui/astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
import DeckSection from "@components/DeckSection.astro";
import { tomVanDeLogtDeck, achiIIesDeck } from "@data/decklists";
---

<Layout
    title="History - Replenish Guide"
    description="Explore the competitive evolution of the Replenish archetype in Magic: The Gathering. Trace its legacy from Type 2 dominance in 2000 to its modern engineering and championship adaptation in Premodern."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="History of the Deck"
        description="The pedigree of Replenish spans more than two decades. From its dominance in Type 2 (Standard) at the turn of the millennium to its modern competitive engineering and adaptation after format bans, here is an anatomical breakdown of the archetype's historical milestones."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/parallax_wave.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Parallax Wave"
        imageCaption="Parallax Wave by Greg Staples"
        isCard={true}
    />
    <div style="margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <Timeline>
            <TimelineItem title="1. The Standard Origins (2000)" titleTag="h2">
                <p>
                    Replenish first asserted itself at the highest echelons of
                    competitive Magic during the 2000 World Championship, where
                    Hall of Famer Tom van de Logt piloted the strategy to a
                    premier Top 8 finish. This classical Type 2 build coupled
                    artifact acceleration like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Sky Diamond">Sky Diamond</a
                    > with active mana denial via <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
                    > to disrupt opposing development. Immense game-to-game consistency
                    was secured through a precise tutoring suite of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Mystical Tutor">Mystical Tutor</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Enlightened Tutor">Enlightened Tutor</a
                    >, while a solitary <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Energy Field">Energy Field</a
                    > acted as an impermeable structural shield until the definitive
                    combo engine could come online.
                </p>

                <Card>
                    <div
                        style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;"
                    >
                        <div>
                            <h3
                                style="margin: 0; font-size: 1.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                            >
                                2000 World Championship (Standard)
                            </h3>
                            <p
                                style="margin: 0.25rem 0 0 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-40);"
                            >
                                Played by Tom van de Logt
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <Grid column={{ md: 3 }} gap="lg">
                        {
                            tomVanDeLogtDeck.columns.map((col) => (
                                <div>
                                    {col.sections.map((sec) => (
                                        <DeckSection
                                            title={sec.title}
                                            items={sec.items}
                                        />
                                    ))}
                                </div>
                            ))
                        }
                    </Grid>
                </Card>
            </TimelineItem>

            <TimelineItem
                title="2. The Parallax Tide Era (Pre-Ban Jan 2026)"
                titleTag="h2"
            >
                <p>
                    As Premodern maturely developed into a competitive eternal
                    format, deckbuilders recognized that <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Tide">Parallax Tide</a
                    > could be weaponized alongside Opalescence to permanently eradicate
                    an opponent's mana base just as efficiently as Wave annihilated
                    creature boards. By late 2025 and early 2026, Parallax Tide Replenish
                    achieved overwhelming metagame dominance. To guarantee unconditional
                    supremacy in contested mirrors and heavy blue battles, elite pilots
                    like AchiIIes evolved the architecture by integrating maindeck
                    copies of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                    > and a definitive four-of suite of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    >—manufacturing an inescapable, unilateral lock right before
                    format curation intervened.
                </p>
                <Card>
                    <div
                        style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;"
                    >
                        <div>
                            <h3
                                style="margin: 0; font-size: 1.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                            >
                                MTGO Premodern League #10124 (Jan 2026)
                            </h3>
                            <p
                                style="margin: 0.25rem 0 0 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-40);"
                            >
                                Played by AchiIIes (Day Before Ban)
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <Grid column={{ md: 3 }} gap="lg">
                        {
                            achiIIesDeck.columns.map((col) => (
                                <div>
                                    {col.sections.map((sec) => (
                                        <DeckSection
                                            title={sec.title}
                                            items={sec.items}
                                        />
                                    ))}
                                </div>
                            ))
                        }
                    </Grid>
                </Card>
            </TimelineItem>

            <TimelineItem
                title="3. The Post-Ban Resurgence (2026 - Present)"
                titleTag="h2"
            >
                <p>
                    On January 18, 2026, format curator Martin Berlin formally
                    banned Parallax Tide, citing repetitive play patterns and
                    oppressive tournament win percentages. Conventional
                    community discourse prematurely dismissed the archetype,
                    assuming Replenish could not survive without its primary
                    resource-denial lock.
                </p>
                <p>
                    However, dedicated specialists engineered an immediate
                    resurgence. By transitioning the primary lock piece to <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                    > and weaving in flexible, agile protection such as <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a
                    >, the deck evolved from a linear combo-lock into an
                    exceptionally formidable combo-control hybrid.
                </p>
                <p>
                    This modern engineering achieved absolute technical
                    validation on May 1, 2026, when
                    <strong>Brian Siu</strong> piloted post-ban Parallax Replenish
                    to conquer the
                    <strong
                        >North American Premodern Championship at Lobstercon
                        2026</strong
                    >, silencing skepticism and cementing the archetype's status
                    as an enduring tournament heavyweight.
                </p>
                <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                    To explore Brian Siu's championship architecture and other
                    refined post-ban configurations, proceed to our interactive <a
                        href="/deck"
                        style="color: var(--w-color-info); text-decoration: underline; font-weight: bold;"
                        >Lists page</a
                    >.
                </p>
            </TimelineItem>
        </Timeline>
    </div>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
